{"hash":"ab3045e788136ad2","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":86.307,"said":"You have climbed to the top of the temple; in the real temple this way is shut on every day of the year but one. Before you is the image, and there is no other like it. Shiva and Parvati sit side by side, Ganesha beside them, and they are seated not on a lotus but on the coils of a great serpent, its hoods spread over them like a canopy. It is usually Vishnu who rests on a serpent; here it is Shiva’s family. Tradition says the image was brought from Nepal; it is generally dated to the eleventh century, the age of the Paramara kings. Now the story devotees tell. Takshak, the king of serpents, once did fierce penance to please Shiva, and Shiva granted him a boon; some say it was immortality. Takshak asked for one thing more: to live near the Lord, but alone, undisturbed. So, it is said, his shrine stays closed, and opens only on Nag Panchami in Shravan, at midnight, for twenty-four hours, while lakhs of devotees climb this way. Now go back down, and come to the stepped tank beside the temple. Its water remembers darker days.\n\nDid you know? On Nag Panchami devotees were once taken up by temporary stairs; today a pedestrian bridge carries the queue to the top floor. Most accounts give the serpent ten hoods; a few say seven.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1038},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1054,"end":1239}],"words":[[0.098,0.168,0,3],[0.278,0.156,4,8],[0.447,0.541,9,16],[1.0,0.096,17,19],[1.108,0.096,20,23],[1.216,0.276,24,27],[1.504,0.096,28,30],[1.613,0.108,31,34],[1.733,0.505,35,41],[2.466,0.12,43,45],[2.598,0.084,46,49],[2.694,0.276,50,54],[2.983,0.409,55,61],[3.403,0.228,62,66],[3.644,0.24,67,70],[3.896,0.132,71,73],[4.04,0.3,74,78],[4.353,0.288,79,81],[4.653,0.361,82,87],[5.026,0.192,88,91],[5.23,0.132,92,94],[5.375,0.132,95,98],[5.519,0.204,99,103],[5.735,0.216,104,107],[5.963,0.385,108,111],[7.236,0.397,113,119],[7.644,0.144,120,123],[7.801,0.132,124,1